  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 231 PM MDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nSynoptically, on Sunday the upper level ridge begins to set up \nover the SE CONUS while a trough begins to move into the Pacific\nNorthwest. This change will transition our upper level flow to \nbe primarily out of the west to southwest. During this period, \nshortwaves are expected to move through the area, potentially \nincreasing precipation chances. \n\nThroughout the period, high temperatures are forecast to be \nmainly in the 90s. Sunday is currently forecast to be the \nwarmest day, with highs in the 90s for the majority of the CWA. \nAreas east of Hwy 83 have the best chance of seeing triple \ndigits temperatures on Sunday. The \"coolest\" day is currently \nforecast to be Monday, with highs in the mid 80s to low 90s. \nWinds are expected to mainly remain calm through the early part \nof the week. \n\nRegarding precipitation, there is potential for showers and \nstorms each afternoon. Sunday and Monday currently have the \nhighest potential with PoPs, ranging from 25-50% across the \narea. As for severe potential, while shear values appear to be \ndecent, guidance suggests that CAPE values may limit storm \ndevelopment. This could result in more showers than storms. From\nTuesday onward, precipitation chances begin to taper off, with \nPoPs in the 10-20% range.",
